// data: nodes, edges (with type), controversies
const N=[
["hester","Frank Hester","money",0,"Tech billionaire — founder of TPP (The Phoenix Partnership), which holds NHS patient-data contracts worth hundreds of millions — and the biggest single donor in British politics: over £15m to the Conservatives, including £10m in a single year (2023–24). In 2024 it emerged he had said MP Diane Abbott made you 'want to hate all black women' and 'should be shot'. PM Sunak called the remarks racist — and kept the money. Why it matters: one man funds the governing party while holding state contracts, and the price of that money is paid in silence about what he says.","Electoral Commission PEF; Guardian; BBC","Frank Hester"],
["marshall","Paul Marshall","money",0,"Hedge-fund billionaire (co-founded Marshall Wace, ~$60bn) who has built a right-wing media empire: co-owner of GB News, owner of UnHerd and The Spectator, and a past bidder for the Telegraph. Gave ~£600k to the Conservatives and £200k to Reform. During the 2024 riots he personally boosted far-right figures on X (including Tommy Robinson and an account calling for 'mass deportations'), then quietly deleted the posts. Why it matters: one ultra-wealthy owner now controls a whole stack of 'anti-establishment' media reaching millions — the rebellion is funded from the top.","Guardian; FT; Electoral Commission","Paul Marshall (investor)"],
["murdoch","Rupert Murdoch","media",0,"For 50 years the most powerful unelected man in Britain: his papers (Sun, Times) have claimed to decide elections ('It's The Sun Wot Won It', 1992) and every PM since Thatcher has courted him. His outlets swung behind Brexit and Boris Johnson. Phone hacking by his tabloids — including a murdered schoolgirl's voicemail — forced the closure of the News of the World and the Leveson Inquiry, yet no senior executive was jailed. Why it matters: he is the template for billionaire media power over democracy, and the phone-hacking scandal shows how long that power operated outside the law.","Leveson Inquiry; Guardian","Rupert Murdoch"],
["harborne","Christopher Harborne","money",0,"A reclusive Thailand-based British billionaire (aviation, defence and crypto — a large shareholder in Tether/Bitfinex) who gave around £20m to the Brexit Party/Reform in 2019–23, then ~$12m (£9m) in 2025 — the largest donation by a living person in UK history. He sued to keep his name off Reform's register and lost. Reform remains a company effectively controlled by Farage rather than a member-owned party. Why it matters: one offshore crypto billionaire is the financial foundation of the UK's fastest-growing party, and fought in court to stay anonymous while doing it.","Electoral Commission; Guardian; openDemocracy","Christopher Harborne"],
["gibb","RedBird IMI / Sheikh Mansour","money",0,"The vehicle — fronted by former CNN chief Jeff Zucker, funded by International Media Investments, ultimately Sheikh Mansour bin Zayed Al Nahyan, UAE vice-president and owner of Manchester City — that agreed in November 2023 to buy the Telegraph and Spectator for £1.2bn by repaying the Barclay family's debts. Parliament responded by banning foreign-state ownership of British newspapers (2024), killing the deal; the Telegraph later went to RedBird Capital, the Spectator to Paul Marshall. Why it matters: an absolute monarchy came within weeks of owning the Conservative party's newspaper — and only legislation, not the market or the regulators, stopped it.","Guardian; FT; DCMS","Telegraph Media Group"],
["tabor","Sir Michael Tabor","money",0,"Billionaire bookmaker — built the Arthur Prince betting-shop chain and sold it for £27m in 1995 — and one of the world's most successful racehorse owners. A long-time Conservative donor. His son Ashley Tabor-King founded Global, the commercial radio giant that owns LBC — the station that gave Nigel Farage his nightly platform for years. Why it matters: a gambling fortune now sits, via the next generation, behind one of the loudest broadcast platforms of the populist right.","Guardian; Companies House","Michael Tabor"],
["reform","Reform UK","party",1,"Britain's fastest-growing party (polling first through 2025–26, five MPs) — and legally not a normal party at all: it is a private company, Reform 2025 Ltd, whose only directors are Nigel Farage and Zia Yusuf. Farage held over 75% control ('person of significant control') until February 2025. Its money: ~£20m from crypto billionaire Christopher Harborne plus a stream of six-figure gifts; the Israeli Ministry of Foreign Affairs paid £50,000 in March 2025 to fly a Reform delegation (Richard Tice) to Israel, and the party launched a 'Jewish Alliance' affiliate in February 2026. In January 2026 it absorbed Nadhim Zahawi, Robert Jenrick and Suella Braverman from the Conservatives. Why it matters: an insurgent 'anti-establishment' party that is owned by its leader, financed by offshore billionaires, courted by a foreign government, and increasingly staffed by the establishment it attacks.","Companies House; Electoral Commission; Guardian; Israeli MFA register","Reform UK"],
["tories","Conservative Party","party",1,"The party of government for 14 years (2010–24), now in opposition and losing MPs to Reform. Its funding model: mega-donors. Frank Hester has given £15m+; Lord Ashcroft ~£10m historically; the 'cash for honours' pattern is documented — 15 of the party's last 16 treasurers (each giving £3m+) were offered peerages. Russian-born donor Alexander Temerko and Lubov Chernukhin (wife of a former Putin minister, £2m+) were welcomed through the Boris Johnson era. Why it matters: a small circle of billionaires and foreign-linked donors has had structural purchase on the party that governed Britain for most of a generation.","Electoral Commission; Guardian; Sunday Times","Conservative Party (UK)"],
["labour","Labour Party","party",1,"In government since 2024 \u2014 Starmer, ground down by the Mandelson and Rayner scandals and catastrophic May 2026 local-election losses to Reform, was forced out and replaced in July 2026 by Andy Burnham, whose route back ran through a Makerfield by-election vacated for him by Josh Simons. Funded by trade unions (Unite etc. give millions and expect policy in return) and increasingly by business: Lord Sainsbury (~£8m historically), Quadrature Capital (£4m in 2024). Blair-era Labour took £1m from Formula 1's Bernie Ecclestone then exempted F1 from the tobacco advertising ban; the 2006 cash-for-honours scandal saw police arrest Blair's fundraiser Lord Levy. Starmer's government has already lost Angela Rayner (stamp duty) and seen Lord Mandelson arrested (Feb 2026) over Epstein-linked leaks. Why it matters: the 'change' government runs on the same donor machinery as its predecessors.","Electoral Commission; BBC; Guardian","Labour Party (UK)"],
["unions","Trade unions","lobby",2,"Labour's founders and still its largest institutional funders — Unite alone has given over £20m since 2010, and union votes decided Labour leadership elections. The relationship is openly transactional: funding follows policy alignment (repeal of anti-union laws, employment rights bill 2024–25). Union barons (McCluskey era Unite) also spent members' money on a Birmingham hotel and conference complex whose costs ballooned past £100m, triggering criminal and civil inquiries. Why it matters: union money buys Labour's direction, and union leadership has its own accountability problems.","Electoral Commission; Guardian; BBC","Unite the Union"],
["lubner","Lord Levy","money",2,"Tony Blair's personal tennis partner and chief fundraiser, ennobled in 1997. Arrested twice in the 2006–07 cash-for-honours police investigation (secret loans to Labour in exchange for peerage nominations) — never charged. His 'blind trust' model let Labour take huge sums without naming donors. Why it matters: he built the template for buying access to a Labour government, and the scandal nearly toppled Blair.","BBC; Guardian","Lord Levy"],
["vince","Sir Vince Cable","money",2,"Liberal Democrat leader (2017–19) whose party ran on a fraction of the big parties' money — their largest donor was Lord Sainsbury (£8m+). The Lib Dems were fined £20,000 in 2016 for election-expenses breaches. Why it matters: even the 'clean' centrist party depended on one billionaire, and still broke funding rules.","Electoral Commission","Vince Cable"],
["sainsbury","Lord Sainsbury","money",2,"Supermarket heir who has given over £40m to British politics — Labour under Blair (£10m+, made Science Minister), then the Lib Dems (£8m+), plus millions to pro-EU campaigns. One of the few donors to operate across party lines, always pro-EU and pro-science funding. Why it matters: shows how a single fortune can steer multiple parties on a single issue (Europe) for 30 years.","Electoral Commission; Guardian","David Sainsbury, Baron Sainsbury of Turville"],
["quadrature","Quadrature Capital","money",2,"A secretive Mayfair quant hedge fund that gave Labour £4m just before the 2024 election — one of the biggest gifts of the campaign — while operating from tax-haven structures and facing litigation from a co-founder over billions in allegedly unpaid share value. Why it matters: Labour's 'change' government was part-financed by offshore quant money with its own legal questions.","Guardian; Electoral Commission","Quadrature Capital"],
["mansour","Sir Mohamed Mansour","money",2,"Egyptian-British billionaire, former transport minister under Mubarak, who gave the Conservatives £5m in 2023 (their biggest gift that year) and was knighted months later. Senior party treasurer. Why it matters: a foreign former regime minister buying a top role inside the Tory party — and an honour — with money.","Electoral Commission; Guardian","Mohamed Mansour (businessman)"],
["cottrell","George Cottrell","person",2,"Farage's 2016 fundraiser-in-chief, a young aristocrat convicted in the US for wire fraud (a money-laundering sting) and later recorded making racist and Holocaust-trivialising remarks — expelled from UKIP in 2019. Remained in Farage's orbit. Why it matters: the money pipeline into the Brexit campaign ran through a convicted fraudster with extremist baggage.","Guardian; BBC","George Cottrell"],
["chandler","Christopher Chandler","money",1,"New Zealand-born billionaire, founder of the Legatum Group (Dubai-based), which funds the Legatum Institute — the think tank credited with shaping the hard-Brexit agenda from inside Whitehall's networks. In 2018 MPs cited MI6-sourced claims about his past business ties to Russia; he sued for libel and won apologies. Legatum's money is offshore and its influence (trade policy 'Legatum model') was vast 2016–19. Why it matters: offshore billionaire money built the intellectual case for Brexit while its source was, for a time, a security question in Parliament.","Guardian; BBC; High Court records","Christopher Chandler"],
["israel_lobby","Pro-Israel lobby","lobby",1,"A network of groups embedded in every major party: Conservative Friends of Israel (CFI — once claimed 80% of Tory MPs as members), Labour Friends of Israel, the Board of Deputies, BICOM, and affiliated 'friends' groups inside Reform (Jewish Alliance, 2026) and UKIP historically. Al Jazeera's undercover film 'The Lobby' (2017) recorded an Israeli embassy officer discussing 'taking down' a minister (Alan Duncan) and funding MPs' groups; Priti Patel resigned in 2017 after undeclared meetings with Israeli officials. In March 2025 Israel's Foreign Ministry paid £50,000 to bring a Reform delegation to Israel. Why it matters: this is the most institutionalised foreign-interest lobby in British politics, operating openly inside all parties — the question is never existence, but degree of influence.","Al Jazeera 'The Lobby' (2017); Israeli MFA donor register; Guardian","Lobbying in the United Kingdom"],
["bbc","BBC","media",1,"The state broadcaster, funded by the licence fee and governed by a board whose chairman and several members are effectively government appointees. Board member Robbie Gibb (ex-Theresa May spin chief, GB News co-founder) is accused by staff of steering coverage; the BBC refuses to call Hamas 'terrorists' in its own voice while platforming government framing on Gaza. Why it matters: whoever controls Downing Street has structural leverage over the country's most trusted news source.","BBC; Guardian; Spectator","BBC"],
["gbnews","GB News","media",1,"A right-wing opinion channel launched June 2021 with ~£60m of start-up money, originally backed by US media giant Discovery, the Dubai-based Legatum group and hedge-fund boss Paul Marshall — Marshall is now co-owner. It pays Nigel Farage a reported £400k–£1m a year to present while he is an MP and party leader, and has employed multiple serving MPs as presenters. Ruled in breach of Ofcom impartiality rules repeatedly (14+ investigations). Why it matters: it is a political party's media arm operating as a licensed news channel — with the party's leader on the payroll.","Ofcom; Guardian; Electoral Commission","GB News"],
["telegraph","The Telegraph","media",1,"The Conservatives' house newspaper for 170 years, whose £1.2bn takeover by UAE-backed RedBird IMI in 2023 was blocked by a new law banning foreign-state newspaper ownership. Long the pipeline between Tory donors, think tanks and front pages. Its 2024–25 election coverage was openly aligned with the party its owner was funding. Why it matters: 'broadsheet respectability' and party apparatus have never been separate here — and the attempted owner was the Abu Dhabi vice-president himself.","Guardian; FT","The Daily Telegraph"],
["restore","Restore Britain","party",2,"Rupert Lowe's political vehicle after his March 2025 expulsion from Reform: he and aide Charlie Downs were smeared as 'nazis and antisemites' (Downs over an old tweet; Lowe over his rhetoric) at the moment they broke with Farage. Restore is the only significant right-wing party without a 'Friends of Israel'-style affiliate. Why it matters: the pattern is consistent — right-wing figures who split from the Farage/Zionist-aligned structure are instantly branded beyond the pale.","Guardian; BBC","Rupert Lowe"],
["farage","Nigel Farage","person",1,"Leader of Reform UK and the most consequential British politician never to hold ministerial office: drove Brexit through UKIP and the Brexit Party, now leads the polls. Simultaneously: party leader, MP for Clacton, and a reported £400k–£1m-a-year presenter on GB News. Controlled Reform as a company (75%+ 'person of significant control' until Feb 2025; still one of two directors). Was debanked by Coutts in 2023 in a scandal that cost the NatWest CEO her job. In January 2026 he executed a reverse takeover of the Tory right — Zahawi, Jenrick, Braverman all defected to him within weeks. Why it matters: he sits at the junction of billionaire money (Harborne, Marshall), media (GB News) and now the ex-Tory establishment — while selling himself as the outsider.","BBC; Guardian; Companies House","Nigel Farage"],
["robinson","Tommy Robinson","person",1,"Street-movement leader (real name Stephen Yaxley-Lennon, EDL founder) who built Britain's largest far-right street movement, UTK, which drew 100,000+ to central London in 2024–25. Convicted multiple times (mortgage fraud, contempt of court, passport offences); lost a 2021 libel case to a Syrian schoolboy and was bankrupted; funded in part by US think tanks (Middle East Forum) and boosted by Elon Musk. Why it matters: the street muscle of the radical right — amplified by billionaires, arrested by the state, and treated as radioactive by the party (Reform) that fishes in the same pond.","BBC; Guardian; court records","Tommy Robinson"],
["musk","Elon Musk","person",1,"The world's richest man used X in 2024–25 as a weapon in British politics: promoted Robinson and UTK during the Southport riots, posted that 'civil war is inevitable' in the UK, demanded Starmer's removal, amplified 'grooming gang' pressure that forced inquiries, then turned on Farage ('Reform needs a new leader') and briefly courted Lowe. Why it matters: a foreign billionaire with a state-adjacent US role tried to choose Britain's government and opposition in real time.","Guardian; BBC","Elon Musk"],
["lbc","LBC","media",2,"Britain's biggest commercial talk station, owned by Global — the radio giant Sir Michael Tabor's son Ashley Tabor-King founded and runs. Gave Farage a nightly show for years (2017–2020) and still platforms his allies; also home to James O'Brien — it sells both sides of the culture war from one building. Why it matters: the 'national conversation' on talk radio is a product of one billionaire family's radio empire.","Guardian","LBC"],
["jc","The Jewish Chronicle","media",2,"The world's oldest Jewish newspaper (founded 1841), which from 2015–20 published a stream of pieces — some later ruled inaccurate by IPSO — that shaped the Labour antisemitism crisis narrative; editor-at-large Stephen Pollard had earlier hired George Cottrell (later convicted fraudster). In 2024 it published fabricated Gaza stories by a fake journalist and had to retract them; ownership passed in 2020 to a consortium including figures linked to the establishment right. Why it matters: a community paper whose editorial line has repeatedly fed national political crises, with accuracy rulings against it in both directions.","IPSO rulings; Guardian; JC itself","The Jewish Chronicle"],
["singer","Paul Singer","money",1,"American billionaire (Elliott Management), the archetypal 'vulture fund' operator — bought distressed Argentine debt for cents and sued for full value, winning a decade-long battle; EU courts ruled against his tactics. A major funder of US Republican and pro-Israel causes (AIPAC-aligned networks, the Foundation for Defense of Democracies) and a donor to British right-wing causes via allied networks. Why it matters: the transatlantic pipeline — US pro-Israel/Republican billionaire money — connects to British politics through the same donor ecosystem.","Guardian; OpenSecrets; ECJ ruling","Paul Singer (businessman)"],
["talk","Talk (TalkTV)","media",2,"Murdoch's TV/radio talk brand (TalkTV folded back to radio/digital in 2024): employed Farage, Richard Tice and Julia Hartley-Brewer; part of News UK alongside the Sun and Times. Why it matters: even the 'insurgent' right's second broadcast home is inside the Murdoch empire.","Guardian","Talk (streaming service)"],
["sky","Sky UK","media",2,"Britain's biggest pay-TV broadcaster, built by Murdoch; his 2016–18 bid for full control was blocked after regulators ruled the deal would give his family too much influence over news (and over Sky News' independence). Now owned by Comcast. Why it matters: the one time the state formally said no to Murdoch — on raw media-power grounds.","CMA report 2018; BBC","Sky UK"],
["spectator","The Spectator","media",2,"The world's oldest weekly magazine, bought in 2024 by Paul Marshall for £100m after being caught in the Telegraph sale. The Tory party's intellectual salon — Johnson, Gove and Farage allies all pass through its pages and its garden parties. Why it matters: the same billionaire who owns GB News now owns the right's ideas factory.","Guardian; FT","The Spectator"],
["uae","United Arab Emirates","lobby",0,"A Gulf absolute monarchy with deep purchase in Britain: owner (via state funds) of Manchester City, major investor in UK infrastructure (SDP, ports), and — through Sheikh Mansour's RedBird IMI vehicle — came within weeks of owning the Telegraph. Used Pegasus spyware to hack the phones of Princess Haya and her British lawyers during her London divorce case, per the High Court (2021). Hired armies of London PR and lobbying firms; funds 'counter-extremism' narratives that align with pro-Israel, anti-Muslim Brotherhood framing. Why it matters: an authoritarian foreign state buying British football, infrastructure, media and influence — with spyware deployed on UK soil.","High Court (2021); Guardian; FT","United Arab Emirates"],
["israel_state","State of Israel","lobby",0,"A foreign state whose official bodies spend directly inside British politics: the Foreign Ministry paid £50,000 for Reform UK figures to visit (2025) and runs open 'hasbara' programmes; its embassy was caught by Al Jazeera (2017) discussing operations against UK ministers. Israeli intelligence alumni (Unit 8200) founded major UK tech/security firms; NSO Group's Pegasus spyware was sold to allies including the UAE. Why it matters: unlike lobby groups, this is a state machine with budget lines, diplomats and intelligence ties operating in London — and no other foreign state is comparably embedded.","Israeli MFA register; Al Jazeera; Guardian","Israel"],
["ashcroft","Lord Ashcroft","money",1,"Belize-based billionaire who bankrolled the Conservatives for decades (~£10m+) and as deputy chairman effectively ran their 2010 marginal-seats campaign — while refusing to confirm his tax status, causing a scandal when his non-dom status emerged. Later reinvented himself as the pollster of record whose surveys shape election coverage. Why it matters: offshore money ran a governing party's election machine, then the same man bought the role of national political umpire.","Guardian; BBC","Michael Ashcroft"],
["chernukhin","Lubov Chernukhin","money",2,"Wife of a former Putin finance minister; the biggest female donor in British history (£2m+ to the Conservatives), paying £160,000 for a tennis match with Boris Johnson and £135,000 for dinner with Theresa May. The party refused to return the money even after the Ukraine invasion, citing her British citizenship. Why it matters: Kremlin-adjacent money bought literal face-time with prime ministers, and the party defended keeping it.","Electoral Commission; Guardian","Lubov Chernukhin"],
["utk","UTK (Unite the Kingdom)","party",2,"Tommy Robinson's street coalition — football-lad networks, far-right activists and disaffected voters — which pulled 100,000–150,000 onto London's streets in 2024–25, the largest far-right mobilisations in British history. Rallies featured arrests, clashes with police and rape-chant signage documented by journalists; Robinson himself was jailed (contempt) mid-movement. Why it matters: the physical force of the radical right — courted by Musk, condemned by Reform, and impossible for the establishment to police away.","BBC; Guardian; court records","English Defence League"],
["tice","Richard Tice","person",1,"Reform's deputy leader (former leader 2019–21, then chairman): a property millionaire who co-founded Leave.EU-adjacent Brexit networks and ran the Brexit Party company (struck off in 2022 with Tice as director). In May 2025 Parliament's standards commissioner opened an investigation after Tice registered shareholdings without formally declaring the interests in debate. Led the Israeli-government-funded Reform delegation to Israel (March 2025). Why it matters: Reform's number two embodies the pattern — personal property wealth, opaque company structures, foreign-government-funded travel, and a standards file.","Parliamentary Commissioner; Israeli MFA register; Companies House","Richard Tice"],
["yusuf","Zia Yusuf","person",1,"Reform's chairman (2024–25) and now head of its 'Doge' unit: a Muslim-born tech millionaire (sold Velocity Black for £233m) who donated £200k and became the party's administrator-in-chief. In March 2025 he made the police complaint that triggered the purge of Rupert Lowe (armed officers seized Lowe's guns; the CPS brought no charges). A BBC investigation (June 2025) reported staff describing his behaviour as 'unpredictable', with frequent dismissals and fear, and alleged he had approved inflated financial figures to his old company's investors (he denied wrongdoing). Quit in June 2025 after a burqa-ban row — and returned within 48 hours. As a Reform 2025 Ltd director he co-controls the party with Farage. Why it matters: the gatekeeper of Britain's insurgent party is a recent Tory donor with a conduct file, total corporate control, and the power to have MPs' doors kicked in.","BBC (June 2025); Companies House; CPS","Zia Yusuf"],
["lowe","Rupert Lowe","person",1,"Southampton FC's former chairman, Reform MP for Great Yarmouth — and the man the machine destroyed. After criticising Farage's 'messianic' leadership (March 2025) he was reported to police by chairman Zia Yusuf over alleged threats; armed officers raided his home and seized his legally held guns; he was suspended from the party and later stripped of the whip. The CPS found no case (June 2025). He then faced a parliamentary standards probe (bullying allegations he calls a stitch-up) and was smeared across GB News and the Telegraph. His answer: Restore Britain, the only right-wing party with no Zionist affiliate. Why it matters: the full playbook — police complaint, guns seized, media pile-on, standards probe — deployed against a dissident within weeks, by his own party.","BBC; CPS statement; Guardian","Rupert Lowe"],
["johnson","Boris Johnson","person",1,"Prime Minister 2019–22: delivered Brexit with Dominic Cummings, then was consumed by his own scandals — Partygate (fined by police for lockdown parties while the country buried relatives alone), the unlawful prorogation of Parliament (Supreme Court, 2019), the attempt to save lobbying MP Owen Paterson that forced his own U-turn, the secret funding of his flat refurbishment, and elevating Evgeny Lebedev to the Lords against security advice. The privileges committee found he deliberately misled Parliament; he resigned rather than face a recall petition. Why it matters: the clearest modern case of power, donors, media (his old Telegraph employers) and impunity converging on one man.","Privileges Committee; Supreme Court; BBC","Boris Johnson"],
["urbanscoop","Urban Scoop","media",2,"A 'citizen journalism' outfit launched 2024 by Brian Rose — the London Real founder expelled from Reform in 2022 over an 'offensive' hand gesture — which became the semi-official media wing of Robinson's UTK rallies, livestreaming to hundreds of thousands and monetising the street movement. Why it matters: the radical right no longer needs broadcasters; it has built its own.","Guardian; Urban Scoop","Brian Rose (podcaster)"],
["zahawi","Nadhim Zahawi","person",1,"Iraqi-born former Chancellor, Education Secretary and — during the pandemic — Vaccines Minister (2020–21), the man who ran Britain's COVID jab rollout. In January 2023 he was sacked as Tory chairman after it emerged he had paid HMRC a settlement reported at ~£5m — including a penalty — over his taxes, negotiated while he was Chancellor, and had lied about it publicly. His YouGov fortune sits in an offshore Gibraltar family trust. On 12 January 2026, reportedly after being denied a peerage, he defected to Reform — the first former Chancellor in their ranks. Why it matters: the man in charge of the COVID response was simultaneously settling a seven-figure tax penalty and denying it — and was recycled into the 'anti-establishment' party the moment it suited him.","BBC; Guardian; HMRC reporting","Nadhim Zahawi"],
["jenrick","Robert Jenrick","person",1,"Former Immigration Minister and Tory leadership runner-up (2024), sacked from the shadow cabinet and defected to Reform on 15 January 2026. As Housing Secretary he unlawfully approved Richard Desmond's £1bn Westferry development in 2020 — rushing it past a planning deadline that saved the Tory donor ~£45m, having sat with him at a fundraiser; the High Court quashed it. As Immigration Minister he oversaw the Afghan relocation scheme during the 2022 data breach that exposed ~19,000 Afghans' details; he says he learned of the breach only after the government gagged it with a superinjunction, then 'strongly opposed' bringing 24,000 affected Afghans to Britain at a cost of £850m — and resigned (Dec 2023) over the Rwanda scheme's weakness. Why it matters: donor favours, secret courts, mass data failure and a secret £850m relocation programme — all under one minister, now Reform's recruit.","High Court; BBC; Guardian (Afghan leak)","Robert Jenrick"],
["braverman","Suella Braverman","person",1,"Twice Home Secretary, twice removed: first (Oct 2022) for emailing classified material from her personal account, then sacked by Sunak (Nov 2023) after accusing the police of pro-Palestinian bias in an unauthorised op-ed. Sacked from Badenoch's shadow cabinet (April 2025), defected to Reform (late January 2026). Campaigned on leaving the ECHR and called pro-Gaza marches 'hate marches'. Why it matters: the hard-right's legal standard-bearer moved from the heart of government to Reform — carrying the 'invasion' framing of immigration with her.","BBC; Guardian","Suella Braverman"],
["expenses","MPs' expenses scandal","scandal",3,"The 2009 Telegraph leak that revealed systematic abuse: moat cleaning, duck houses, flipped second homes and mortgage fraud across all parties. Several MPs jailed; hundreds forced to repay. It shattered public trust and fuelled the anti-politics wave that UKIP and later Reform rode. Why it matters: the original proof that Westminster's rules were honoured mainly in the breach — the seedbed of today's 'they're all at it' politics.","Telegraph (2009); IPSA records",null],
["cashhonours","Cash for honours","scandal",3,"The recurring sale of seats in the legislature: 2006–07 police probe into secret Labour loans (Lord Levy arrested; Blair questioned); the documented Tory pattern — 15 of the last 16 party treasurers who gave £3m+ received peerages; Boris Johnson's 2020 elevation of Evgeny Lebedev against MI5 advice. Why it matters: membership of Britain's upper house has a going rate, and everyone in Westminster knows it.","BBC; Guardian; Sunday Times",null],
["partygate","Partygate","scandal",3,"While COVID rules banned funerals and family visits, Downing Street held at least 16 gatherings — including the night before Prince Philip's isolated funeral. 126 police fines, including to the PM and Chancellor; Johnson found to have deliberately misled Parliament. Why it matters: the moment millions concluded the rules were for them, not for the rulers — the single biggest trust-destroyer of the era.","Met Police; Privileges Committee; Sue Gray report",null],
["covidcontracts","COVID PPE contracts","scandal",3,"£15bn+ in pandemic contracts awarded through a 'VIP lane' fast-tracking companies referred by ministers, MPs, peers and donors: PPE Medpro (£200m, linked to peer Michelle Mone — now under NCA investigation, £75m assets restrained), Randox (Owen Paterson lobbying), Ayanda (£155m, adviser Andrew Mills). The High Court ruled the VIP lane unlawful (2022). Much of the PPE was unusable. Why it matters: the biggest documented transfer of public money to the politically connected in modern British history.","High Court (2022); NAO; Good Law Project",null],
["zahawitax","Zahawi tax affair","scandal",3,"The Chancellor of the Exchequer — the man in charge of HMRC — secretly settled his own tax bill with a penalty (reported total ~£5m) over YouGov shares held in a Gibraltar trust, denied the story as 'smears' while threatening journalists with libel action, and was sacked when the ministerial code breach became undeniable (Jan 2023). Why it matters: the taxman's boss was paying tax penalties and lying about it — and the system would never have known without the press.","BBC; Guardian; Dan Neidle investigation",null],
["robinsonlibel","Robinson libel & bankruptcy","scandal",3,"Robinson's 2018 claims that Syrian refugee schoolboy Jamal Hijazi had attacked girls were proven fabricated; he lost the 2021 libel trial (representing himself), was ordered to pay £100k plus ~£1.5m costs, declared bankruptcy — and was later found by the court to have hidden assets and broken court orders, leading to contempt jail time. Why it matters: the legal paper trail behind the 'they tried to silence him' narrative — what the courts actually found.","High Court (2021); BBC",null],
["gaza_doc","Gaza documentary row","scandal",3,"In 2025 the BBC pulled its documentary 'Gaza: How to Survive a War Zone' after revelations that its child narrator was the son of a Hamas minister and that production payments reached the family — sparking an internal review and Ofcom scrutiny, and feeding the long war over BBC Gaza coverage from both sides. Why it matters: a case study in how the most contested conflict on earth distorts Britain's state broadcaster in both directions at once.","BBC; Ofcom; Guardian",null],
["phonehack","Phone hacking","scandal",3,"News of the World journalists hacked thousands of phones — celebrities, politicians, murdered schoolgirl Milly Dowler, 7/7 victims' families. The paper was closed (2011), Andy Coulson jailed, Rebekah Brooks acquitted; civil settlements have cost News Group over £1bn and continue (Prince Harry settlement 2025). The Leveson Inquiry's second part — into corruption between press, police and politicians — was cancelled by the government. Why it matters: Britain's biggest media company ran a criminal intelligence operation for years, and the inquiry into who protected it was shut down.","Leveson Inquiry; Guardian; court records",null],
["ofcom","Ofcom vs GB News","scandal",3,"Ofcom has ruled GB News in breach of impartiality rules repeatedly — including for letting serving politicians act as news presenters — yet imposed no licence sanction, as fines and 'special measures' stalled. GB News calls the regulator censorious; critics call it toothless. Why it matters: a live test of whether Britain's broadcast rules can survive a channel owned by the very people the rules are meant to constrain.","Ofcom rulings 2023–25; Guardian",null],
["jc_fab","The JC's fabricated Gaza stories","scandal",3,"In 2024 the Jewish Chronicle published nine articles by 'Elon Perry' — a journalist who did not exist — containing invented claims about Hamas and the IDF, sourced from what turned out to be a fabricated biography. Israeli media exposed the fraud; the JC retracted the stories, which by then had been cited internationally. Earlier, in the 2019 election, the paper took the unprecedented step of a front-page appeal to non-Jewish readers to vote against Corbyn over antisemitism. Why it matters: a community paper twice broke its own rules of evidence — once into the heart of a general election, once into a war — and the retractions never travelled as far as the stories.","IPSO; Guardian; JC retractions",null],
["lebedev","Lebedev peerage","scandal",3,"Boris Johnson nominated Evgeny Lebedev — son of ex-KGB officer Alexander Lebedev, owner of the Evening Standard and Independent — to the House of Lords in 2020 despite security services' advice, reportedly intervening personally to override it. The Lebedevs had hosted Johnson at their Italian villa while he was Foreign Secretary. Why it matters: a peerage for a KGB-linked media owner, forced through against intelligence advice, by a PM who partied at his villa.","Guardian; BBC; Intelligence & Security Committee",null],
["grooming","Grooming gangs inquiries","scandal",3,"Decades of organised child sexual exploitation in Rotherham, Rochdale, Telford and elsewhere — overwhelmingly by British-Pakistani men (Jay, Casey reports) — went unprosecuted as police and councils feared 'community tensions' accusations. Musk's January 2025 intervention forced it back to the top of politics: Labour whipped MPs to kill the Tories' inquiry amendment 364–111, then U-turned and ordered one after the Casey audit (June 2025). The £65m statutory inquiry has since stumbled — its chair gone, survivors resigned — Farage demands Parliament seize it, and Rupert Lowe's own crowdfunded inquiry published a 219-page first report across 149 districts (2026). In London the Met is reviewing 9,000 cases, with 4,000+ flagged for possible reinvestigation. Why it matters: the state's admitted failure to protect thousands of children to avoid being called racist — and the proof that a foreign billionaire's posts now set Britain's political agenda.","Jay Report (2014); Casey audit (2025); ITV; Evening Standard",null],
["blair","Tony Blair","person",1,"Prime Minister 1997\u20132007, architect of New Labour \u2014 and out of office more structurally powerful than ever. His Tony Blair Institute for Global Change (TBI) is by far Britain's largest think tank (~1,000 staff, 45 countries, $145m turnover), bankrolled since 2021 by Oracle founder Larry Ellison's foundation (\u00a3257m+ donated or pledged) while it lobbies ministers on AI and national data policy that mirrors Oracle's commercial interests. His people sat at the heart of Starmer's government \u2014 his former chief of staff Jonathan Powell was appointed National Security Adviser in 2024 \u2014 and No 10 was staffed by Blair-machine veterans. Why it matters: the foundations he poured \u2014 donor-funded Labour, media management, technocratic centrism \u2014 still define what is politically possible, and his institute is helping write the current government's AI playbook.","New Statesman / Democracy for Sale TBI investigation (2025); gov.uk; The Independent","Tony Blair"],
["fabian","Fabian Society","lobby",2,"Founded 1884, the intellectual engine of the British left: it co-founded the Labour Representation Committee that became the Labour Party and remains constitutionally affiliated to Labour as a socialist society. Every Labour prime minister has been a Fabian. Today: 140+ Fabian MPs elected in 2024, around half the Cabinet, with Starmer and Reeves both former executive-committee members and Wes Streeting a vice chair; its policy work (National Care Service, workers' rights) feeds directly into the government's programme. Why it matters: a 140-year-old members' society most voters have never heard of sits inside the governing party's bloodstream \u2014 gradualism as a strategy, power through permeation rather than election.","Fabian Society (fabians.org.uk); Britannica","Fabian Society"],
["hnh","Hope Not Hate","lobby",3,"Britain's largest anti-fascist organisation, founded in 2004 by former Searchlight editor Nick Lowles in response to the rise of the BNP. Its researchers infiltrate and expose far-right networks \u2014 undercover work dramatised by ITV as The Walk In \u2014 and it documents and campaigns against everyone on the far right of this map, from Tommy Robinson to Reform's hard edge. Why it matters: the far right does not operate unopposed \u2014 its every move is watched, recorded and published by Britain's full-time anti-fascist intelligence operation.","hopenothate.org.uk; New Internationalist; The Conduit","Hope Not Hate"],
["burnham","Andy Burnham","person",1,"Mayor of Greater Manchester 2017\u201326 ('King of the North'), former Blair and Brown cabinet minister and two-time leadership runner-up \u2014 now Prime Minister (20 July 2026). Blocked by the NEC from the Gorton and Denton by-election in January 2026, he returned via Makerfield in June after Josh Simons stood aside for him; Starmer resigned four days later and Burnham was acclaimed leader unopposed, nominated by 379 of 403 Labour MPs. The first Labour PM who is also a Co-operative Party member.","ITV News; labour.org.uk; The Guardian","Andy Burnham"],
["islam","Islam in Britain","lobby",0,"3.9 million Muslims (6.5% of the UK, 2021 census), concentrated in urban seats — an electorate that makes and breaks MPs. In 2019 over 80% voted Labour; in 2024 the Gaza backlash cut Labour's share by 29 points in seats over 30% Muslim and carried five pro-Gaza independents (Shockat Adam, Ayoub Khan, Adnan Hussain, Iqbal Mohamed and Jeremy Corbyn) into Parliament, where they formed the Independent Alliance. Shabana Mahmood's Ladywood vote share fell ~40 points; Wes Streeting clung on by 528 votes. Whoever speaks for this bloc holds a veto over dozens of seats — and everyone from Labour to the Muslim Brotherhood knows it.","UK Election Analysis 2024; The New Arab; Muslim News","Islam in the United Kingdom"],
["pakistan","Pakistan","lobby",0,"The state behind Britain's largest Pakistani diaspora — around 1.6 million people, the biggest in Europe. Its stake in Westminster runs through Kashmir politics (dedicated friendship groups in both main parties) and 'biraderi' clan-network voting in dozens of urban seats, while remittances flow the other way. Its diaspora sits at the very top of British power: the Home Secretary's family is from Mirpur, Azad Kashmir, and the Mayor of London's parents emigrated from Pakistan.","House of Commons Library; geo.tv; London Museum","Pakistan–United Kingdom relations"],
["qatar","Qatar","lobby",0,"The Gulf gas state that quietly bought pieces of the capital: Harrods (£1.5bn, 2010), the Shard, Canary Wharf, Chelsea Barracks and a slice of Heathrow, via its sovereign wealth fund. Simultaneously accused by Saudi Arabia, the UAE, Egypt and Bahrain — who blockaded it in 2017 — of being the Muslim Brotherhood's state sponsor and Hamas's host, charges Doha denies. Its state broadcaster Al Jazeera beams into every British home.","The Guardian; Middle East Eye; meforum.org","Qatar–United Kingdom relations"],
["mb","Muslim Brotherhood network","lobby",2,"Founded in Egypt in 1928, the Brotherhood's British milieu was mapped by a UK government review (Jenkins/Farr, published December 2015): membership, fundraising and education programmes 'secretive'; the Muslim Association of Britain 'dominated by the Muslim Brotherhood'; UKIM, FOSIS, the ISB, the Islamic Forum of Europe and the MCB named as associated organisations. The review stopped short of a ban — no UK violence found, but a network helping create the conditions in which extremism flourishes. The UAE demanded the crackdown, threatening arms deals, investment and intelligence cooperation, and even pressed London to curb BBC reporting; Qatar is accused of being its sponsor (denied).","gov.uk (Jenkins review, 2015); The Guardian; MEE","2014 UK government review of the Muslim Brotherhood"],
["khan","Sadiq Khan","person",1,"Mayor of London since 2016 — the first Muslim mayor of a Western capital and the first London mayor to win three terms (2024). Son of a Pakistani bus driver, raised on a Wandsworth council estate; human-rights lawyer, then MP for Tooting. Knighted, and in July 2026 elevated to the House of Lords in Starmer's outgoing peerages — while still Mayor. Long-running feud with Donald Trump; performed the hajj in 2026.","gov.uk political peerages July 2026; The Telegraph; London Museum","Sadiq Khan"],
["mahmood","Shabana Mahmood","person",1,"Home Secretary (September 2025, reappointed by Burnham July 2026); Lord Chancellor 2024–25 — the first Muslim woman to hold a great office of state. Born Birmingham 1980 to parents from Mirpur, Azad Kashmir; childhood years in Taif, Saudi Arabia; Oxford-educated barrister; MP for Birmingham Ladywood since 2010. The two records: in opposition she voted against a stricter asylum system 19 times (2015–26) and signed the 2020 letter to halt a Jamaica deportation flight; in office she authored the harshest asylum package in a generation (30-month refugee leave, 20-year settlement, £10k 'refugee tax'), kept the Palestine Action ban through the courts, de-proscribed HTS, and abolished non-crime hate incidents. Told The Times (Aug 2026) she will keep spreading asylum accommodation 'fairly' around the country — dispersal contracts tripled to £15bn+, hotel exit slipped to 2029. As NEC chair her panel blocked Burnham's Gorton & Denton bid (Jan 2026), then she announced his leadership win (Jul 2026) — and kept the Home Office. 2024 majority cut from 23,868 to 3,421 by Gaza independent Akhmed Yakoob. Why it matters: she holds MI5, police, Prevent, proscription and borders — and her voting record and her governing record point in opposite directions. Full timeline: /app/#/mahmood","Hansard; TheyWorkForYou; The Times; gov.uk; Full Fact","Shabana Mahmood"],
["aljazeera","Al Jazeera English","media",3,"Qatar's state-funded global news channel — Doha money, London newsroom. Its 2017 undercover series The Lobby secretly filmed an Israeli embassy official and exposed Labour Friends of Israel's back-channel to the embassy; Ofcom rejected complaints, but the fallout reshaped how the Israel lobby operates in Britain.","Al Jazeera; Declassified UK; The Guardian","Al Jazeera English"],
["wef","World Economic Forum","lobby",0,"Founded by Klaus Schwab in 1971 and funded by around 1,000 multinational corporations, the Davos summit is where governments, central bankers and billionaires settle the agenda before voters ever hear it. Schwab quit as chair in April 2025 amid whistleblower allegations (an internal probe cleared him of 'material wrongdoing'); BlackRock's Larry Fink now co-chairs the Forum — and in February 2026 its president Børge Brende resigned over his Epstein links. Its British congregation runs deep: Tony Blair sat on its board of trustees, Starmer and Reeves are Davos attendees, Khan an agenda contributor. Why it matters: no ballot box, no manifesto — yet every government on this map sends its people to the mountain.","FT; Politico; The Telegraph; weforum.org","World Economic Forum"],
["mandelson","Peter Mandelson","person",1,"'The Prince of Darkness' — architect of New Labour alongside Blair and Brown, twice forced out of the cabinet (1998 loan scandal, 2001 Hinduja affair), EU trade commissioner, then brought back by Starmer as ambassador to Washington in 2025. Sacked within months when his supportive emails to Jeffrey Epstein ('I think the world of you') surfaced, and arrested in February 2026 on suspicion of misconduct in public office over alleged leaks to Epstein. Why it matters: his fall is the scandal that broke Starmer's government — and the clearest thread from Epstein's network into the heart of British power.","BBC; The Guardian; The Times","Peter Mandelson"],
["campbell","Alastair Campbell","person",2,"Tony Blair's director of communications 1997–2003 — the most feared spin doctor of his era, central to the Iraq 'dodgy dossier' row and the Dr David Kelly tragedy. Expelled from Labour in 2019 after admitting he voted Liberal Democrat, he reinvented himself as co-host of The Rest Is Politics, Britain's biggest political podcast. Why it matters: the media-management machine he and Mandelson built for New Labour is the template every party on this map still runs on.","The Guardian; BBC; Goalhanger","Alastair Campbell"],
["brown","Gordon Brown","person",1,"Chancellor 1997–2007 — the longest-serving of the modern era — then Prime Minister 2007–10. Sold half Britain's gold reserves near historic lows, light-touch-regulated the City into the 2008 crash, then led the bank bailouts and global response credited with stopping a depression. His decade-long 'TB-GBs' feud with Blair's camp defined an era. Why it matters: the post-crash settlement he shaped — bailouts, QE, the austerity trigger — is still the ground every government on this map fights on.","The Guardian; UK Parliament records","Gordon Brown"],
["archer","Jeffrey Archer","person",3,"Best-selling novelist and life peer — once deputy chairman of the Conservative Party, who quit the 1999 London mayoral race and was jailed for four years in 2001 for perjury and perverting the course of justice over his 1987 libel win against the Daily Star. Why it matters: a walking emblem of the era when Tory grandees, newspaper money and libel law mixed — and of how long the establishment protects its own before the fall.","BBC; The Guardian","Jeffrey Archer"],
["epstein","Jeffrey Epstein","person",1,"American financier and convicted sex offender whose 'black book' ran through the circles of princes, presidents and prime ministers. Convicted in Florida in 2008 on a condemned 'sweetheart' deal, arrested on federal sex-trafficking charges in July 2019 and found dead in his Manhattan cell a month later. His reach into Britain is still detonating: Andrew Mountbatten-Windsor lost his titles, Peter Mandelson was sacked as ambassador and arrested, and the WEF's president resigned over their meetings. Why it matters: the only dead node on this map — and none explains 2026's casualties better.","US DOJ; BBC; House Oversight Epstein-files releases","Jeffrey Epstein"],
["bannon","Steve Bannon","person",2,"Trump's 2016 campaign chief and White House strategist, Breitbart chairman and War Room host — the connective tissue of the transatlantic populist right. He championed Tommy Robinson ('the backbone of this country'), filmed hours coaching Jeffrey Epstein for a never-aired 60 Minutes rehabilitation, and was jailed in 2024 for contempt of Congress. Why it matters: through Robinson and Farage, his influence operation reaches straight into Britain's street movements and its insurgent right.","New York Times; Hollywood Reporter; Middle East Eye Epstein-files reporting","Steve Bannon"],
["ellison","Larry Ellison","money",0,"Founder of Oracle — briefly the world's richest man (September 2025). His foundation has donated or pledged £257m+ to the Tony Blair Institute since 2021, growing it to ~1,000 staff across 45 countries, while Oracle has won £1.1bn+ in UK public-sector contracts since 2022 and TBI pushes national AI and data policy mirroring Oracle's commercial interests. Former staff describe institute and company as 'inseparable'. Why it matters: a single foreign billionaire bankrolls the think tank writing Britain's AI playbook.","New Statesman / Democracy for Sale investigation (2025)","Larry Ellison"],
["fink","Larry Fink","money",0,"Chairman and CEO of BlackRock — the world's largest asset manager, stewarding ~$11.6 trillion — and interim co-chair of the World Economic Forum since 2025. Courted at the top of British politics: a BlackRock investment roundtable with Starmer and Reeves in No 10 (November 2024), then a Downing Street growth summit (May 2025). Why it matters: when the government says 'growth, growth, growth', this is the man it is pitching to.","City AM; openDemocracy 'The BlackRock letters'","Larry Fink"],
["thiel","Peter Thiel","money",1,"PayPal mafia don and Palantir co-founder/chairman — the surveillance-tech billionaire whose company built the NHS's £330m Federated Data Platform (awarded 2023) after years of lobbying and what he himself called 'buying its way in'. Palantir also holds MoD contracts and a 'strategic partnership' with Israel's military (2024); Thiel has said the NHS 'makes people sick'. Why it matters: Britain's most sensitive health data now sits on the platform of a man openly hostile to the institution.","BMJ; The Conversation; Democracy for Sale","Peter Thiel"],
["mittal","Sunil Mittal","money",1,"Indian telecoms tycoon — founder and chairman of Bharti Enterprises (Airtel), and since 2024 the largest shareholder in BT Group (24.95%, bought from Patrick Drahi's debt-strained Altice and cleared under the National Security and Investment Act). He joined BT's board in September 2025; in 2026 the government moved to block him going further. Why it matters: Britain's former national telecoms champion — critical infrastructure — now has its biggest voice seated from New Delhi.","BT Group; Telecoms.com; Mobile Europe","Sunil Mittal"],
];
